WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.136
Good morning, trainees!

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:06.439
Today we start the heart of the job: checking for alerts.

3
00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:10.364
In this demonstration it is nine thirty on my shift.

4
00:00:10.364 --> 00:00:19.197
I open the SOC alert queue, compare every alert with the time limits in our SOP, and decide what I must handle first.

5
00:00:19.197 --> 00:00:25.311
The demo alerts are different from the ones in Activity 5.1, so watch the method.

6
00:00:26.411 --> 00:00:27.726
First, the SOP.

7
00:00:27.726 --> 00:00:33.511
SOC-SOP-01, rule A2, says how fast I must acknowledge a new alert.

8
00:00:33.511 --> 00:00:43.854
Critical within fifteen minutes, High within thirty, Medium within one hundred twenty, Low within four hundred eighty.

9
00:00:43.854 --> 00:00:47.710
Informational alerts need no acknowledgment.

10
00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.567
Waiting time is now minus the time received.

11
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:54.371
Over the limit is an SLA breach.

12
00:00:55.471 --> 00:00:59.421
Now the demo queue on the Windows workstation.

13
00:00:59.421 --> 00:01:08.435
Import-Csv reads the export, and Format-Table shows only the columns I need: ID, time received, severity,

14
00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:09.808
host and status.

15
00:01:09.808 --> 00:01:10.753
Six alerts.

16
00:01:10.753 --> 00:01:14.959
Two are already Acknowledged, four are still New.

17
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:19.912
Let me check the two acknowledged alerts first.

18
00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:25.403
D5-01 is Medium: received eight oh four, acknowledged eight thirty.

19
00:01:25.403 --> 00:01:29.583
Twenty-six minutes, well within one hundred twenty.

20
00:01:29.583 --> 00:01:36.057
D5-02 is Critical: received eight fifteen forty, acknowledged eight thirty-six.

21
00:01:36.057 --> 00:01:42.040
That is almost twenty-one minutes, but the limit for Critical is fifteen.

22
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:47.860
That acknowledgment was late, an SLA breach, and I tell the Shift Lead.

23
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.968
Now only the New alerts.

24
00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:55.320
Where-Object keeps the rows where status equals New.

25
00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.182
It is nine thirty.

26
00:01:59.182 --> 00:02:07.383
D5-03 is Low and has waited thirty-eight minutes: fine, the limit is eight hours.

27
00:02:07.383 --> 00:02:15.381
D5-04 is High and has waited twenty-six minutes: only about three minutes left!

28
00:02:15.381 --> 00:02:19.735
D5-05 is High and has waited eight minutes.

29
00:02:19.735 --> 00:02:24.696
D5-06 is Informational: no acknowledgment needed.

30
00:02:25.796 --> 00:02:28.233
But time is not the only rule.

31
00:02:28.233 --> 00:02:32.946
Rule A4 says red flags go first, whatever the queue order.

32
00:02:32.946 --> 00:02:40.340
On Linux I search the queue for the two red flag families in the SOP: ransomware and virus.

33
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.452
Here is my receipt log.

34
00:02:43.452 --> 00:02:51.500
D5-05 is a red flag, a file-infecting virus, so I escalate it to the Shift Lead immediately.

35
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:57.799
Then, in the same minute, I acknowledge D5-04 before its limit runs out.

36
00:02:57.799 --> 00:03:03.049
D5-02 was already handled, but late, so I report the breach.

37
00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:09.348
Notice that I did not close, delete, or change the severity of anything.

38
00:03:09.348 --> 00:03:12.760
Assessment and ticketing come tomorrow.

39
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:14.727
Your turn.

40
00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:19.415
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 5 and Activity 5.1.

41
00:03:19.415 --> 00:03:22.105
Your queue has thirteen alerts.

42
00:03:22.105 --> 00:03:30.438
For each one, work out the minutes, decide whether it is within the SLA, and mark the red flags.

43
00:03:30.438 --> 00:03:35.820
Then answer: which alert must you handle first at nine thirty?

